<TopicPage slug="subjects/earth-science/advanced/geochemical-cycles">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Geochemical cycles track <strong>every element\'s journey through Earth\'s reservoirs</strong>
    — the planet\'s material accounting. The discipline\'s insight: the cycles interlock, run at
    different speeds, and are now measurably perturbed by one species (the "Great Acceleration"
    of the Anthropocene curves).
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the loop logic, the timescale stack, nitrogen, phosphorus" widget="equationStep" config={geochemCfg} />

  <h2>The human fingerprint, measured</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Carbon (the carbon-cycle lesson\'s detail):</strong> we move ~10 GtC/year from the geological reservoir into the fast one — the ocean absorbs ~25%, land ~30%, the rest accumulates: an isotope signature (fossil carbon is ancient carbon) proves the source.</li>
    <li><strong>Sulfur and acid rain — the solved case:</strong> industrial SO₂ acidified lakes and forests; cap-and-trade (1990 US Clean Air Act) cut emissions ~90% while the economy grew — environmental diplomacy\'s template (the environmental-economics lesson).</li>
    <li><strong>Oxygen: the big buffer:</strong> atmospheric O₂ (~21%) turns over on ~2,000 years via photosynthesis–respiration — deforestation barely dents it. The worry is ocean deoxygenation (expanding dead zones), not the air\'s oxygen.</li>
    <li><strong>The planetary boundaries framework:</strong> Rockström\'s team rates nine cycles (nitrogen and phosphorus among the most exceeded) — contested numbers, but the first serious attempt to put gauges on Earth systems.</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Schlesinger & Bernhardt, Biogeochemistry; Steffen et al., "Planetary Boundaries" (2015); Smil, Enriching the Earth (2001).</p>
</TopicPage>
